XPENG, best known for electric cars, used its AI Day to set out dates for IRON, its humanoid robot. One of two company releases datelined 25 November 2026 says hardware mass production is planned to begin in January 2027. This explainer separates what has a date, what is only a company claim, and what a business or household still cannot find out.

*Disclosure: this post was drafted by Claude, an AI assistant made by Anthropic, and checked by a separate AI reviewer against the cited pages for figures, dates and wording. This is not a full human review. Stackbrook did not test the robot and has no affiliate relationship with any company mentioned at the time of writing. The header image is AI-generated.*

## The dated plan

Taken together, the two November releases set out these next steps:

- **January 2027:** hardware mass production is planned to begin (IRON release).
- **First quarter of 2027:** IRON is due to train as a store assistant in XPENG's own retail shops (both releases).
- **Second quarter of 2027:** an official public release is targeted (AI Day release).

The company says it wants to develop and check the robot's abilities in that retail role before adding others. It lists front-desk reception, store training, education programmes, livestream hosting, event presentation and exhibition guidance as potential applications. It also says it is exploring commercial services, education and industrial work. "Potential" and "exploring" are not launches.

![Timeline chart of XPENG's stated IRON dates, comparing the 8 September 2026 page with the 25 November 2026 releases](/images/site1/xpeng-iron-humanoid-robot-timeline-explained-chart.svg)
*Chart created by Stackbrook from the cited pages. Bars show the periods XPENG named, not exact days; the left end of the first bar is simply where the chart begins.*

## The September wording was different

On 8 September 2026, XPENG announced that its humanoid robot production lines had been commissioned and that a first IRON had walked off the production line. That page said XPENG robots were scheduled to enter mass production by the end of that year, with first commercial rollouts in the company's stores and campuses. It put official market launch and delivery, in China and overseas, in 2027.

The November release about IRON gives January 2027 for the start of hardware mass production, and its quote from the chief executive says mass production without the word hardware. We found no explanation of the difference in either November release. The two statements may use "mass production" for slightly different milestones, so we do not call it a confirmed delay. It is still a good reason to treat each date as a plan that can move.

## What XPENG says the robot can do

The figures below are the company's own. The pages we read do not present independent test results.

| Item | What XPENG states |
| --- | --- |
| Movement | 76 degrees of freedom (independent ways to move), with 21 in each dexterous hand according to the September page |
| Touch | More than 1,500 tactile sensing points, covering more than 80% of key body areas |
| Sight | 3D vision and 360-degree perception |
| Computing | Three in-house Turing AI chips, up to 2,250 TOPS (trillion operations per second) of effective computing power |
| Daily running | Autonomous navigation, task lists by voice or app, self-charging and reporting of abnormal conditions |

One release gives a target of 24-hour availability. A target is not a measured uptime figure, and we found no battery capacity, run time or charging time on any of the three pages.

The software is described in three layers. A slower one plans tasks, a real-time one built on XPENG's VLA 2.0 model (vision, language and action) acts on what the robot sees, and a very fast one controls the body. The September page says the company's AI model runs directly on the robot, which XPENG says allows tasks without remote operation, with low delay and better data security.

## Safety claims and open questions

XPENG says IRON has five layers of protection. The features it mentions include collision detection, torque limits, an emergency stop, two redundant communication channels, a separate fail-safe balance controller and automatic emergency braking to protect pedestrians. The September page also describes an enclosed flexible lattice structure and says the robot is designed to meet the highest safety standards.

That last phrase names no standard. The pages we read cite no certification body or test report. A robot that walks near customers raises practical questions, so any business considering a trial should ask:

1. Which safety standards has this exact model been certified against, and by whom?
2. What happens if it loses power or connection while moving?
3. What do its cameras and microphones record, where is that stored, and for how long?
4. Who is liable if it injures someone or damages property?
5. Which tasks has it done unsupervised, and what was the failure rate?

This is general information, not legal advice.

## Factory scale and business model

XPENG says its automated line has more than 600 AI-powered holographic quality checks, plus more than 1,000 vehicle-grade tests covering whole robots and key parts. The company gives the design rate as one robot every 2.5 minutes when running at full capacity, which works out to 24 an hour. That is a design figure, not a report of current output or of orders. It also reports a data-collection site called the Robot Data Factory, 50,000 square metres in size, with 15 job categories and more than 250 scenarios.

On money, XPENG names hardware sales, software subscriptions and an open robotics ecosystem. Token-based subscriptions and services tied to particular uses are still being explored. Its September page also said that on 24 August the robotics business had signed agreements to raise over US$900 million at a post-money valuation above US$6.3 billion. This is not investment advice.

## What this means for you

If you run a small shop, office or venue, the pages we read give no price or way to order. XPENG does describe business buyers: the IRON release says customers purchase the robot for use in commercial and industrial settings, and the September page put market launch and delivery in 2027. We found no list of sales countries beyond the general mention of China and overseas markets, and no consumer or home-use date. The September page quotes the chief executive hoping robots will become part of everyday life, but that is an ambition, not a product listing.

The useful checkpoints are simple. Watch whether mass production is confirmed as started in January, whether robots appear working in XPENG shops in the first quarter, and whether the public release in the second quarter comes with a price, safety certificates and run-time figures. Until then, do not build 2027 staffing or budgets around it, and ask any seller for written evidence before paying a deposit.
